
\Zb#==================================================================================================#
\Zb#==================================================================================================#
\Zb
\ZbA vdev (virtual device) is a fundamental building block of ZFS storage pools.
\ZbIt represents a logical grouping of physical storage devices, such as hard drives, SSDs, or partitions.
\Zb
\ZbOpenZFS supports several types of vdevs. Top-level vdevs carry data and provide redundancy:
\Zb
\Zb- ZFS single device: one drive.
\Zb    Standard performance
\Zb    No redundancy.
\Zb
\Zb- ZFS Stripe: One or more devices striped together (like RAID 0)
\Zb    Provides maximum performance but no redundancy. Data will be lost if any device fails.
\Zb
\Zb
\Zb- ZFS Mirror: Stores identical data on two drives for redundancy.
\Zb    Read performance is roughly doubled, write performance equals a single drive.
\Zb    Half the storage capacity is usable.
\Zb
\Zb- ZFS Stripe + Mirror: Two mirror vdevs striped together. Requires minimum 4 drives.
\Zb    Offers roughly 4x read performance with write performance equivalent to two drives.
\Zb    Half the storage capacity.
\Zb
\Zb----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\ZbRAIDZ: A vdev that uses parity to provide fault tolerance, similar to traditional RAID 5/6.
\Zb    There are three levels of RAIDZ:
\Zb
\Zb- RAIDZ1: Single parity (similar to RAID 5). Minimum 3 disks, recommended 3+.
\Zb- RAIDZ2: Double parity (similar to RAID 6). Minimum 5 disks, recommended 5+.
\Zb- RAIDZ3: Triple parity. Minimum 7 disks, recommended 7+.
\Zb----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\Zb
\Zb#==================================================================================================#
\Zb#                                   Nordix Performance Guide                                       #
\Zb#==================================================================================================#
\Zb
\Zb- ZFS single drive: gives you one drive performance
\Zb    Performance = Standard disk latency
\Zb    Redundancy = 0
\Zb
\Zb- ZFS Stripe goes fast - it offers the absolute highest performance but provides no redundancy.
\Zb    All storage capacity is usable.
\Zb    Performance = increases with each drive, though not linearly.
\Zb    Total throughput rises, but efficiency per drive decreases slightly due to stripe overhead.
\Zb    from one drive to two drives, performance doubles,
\Zb    and three drives almost three times as fast as a single drive.
\Zb    Going beyond three drives and you will start noticing stripe overhead.
\Zb    The performance will increase but not as good as it does in the beginning.
\Zb    Redundancy = 0.
\Zb
\Zb* ZFS Mirror offers roughly double the read performance with write performance equivalent
\Zb    to a single drive. Half the storage capacity is usable.
\Zb    Redundancy: can lose 1 drive.
\Zb
\Zb* ZFS Stripe + Mirror offers roughly four times the read performance
\Zb    with write performance equivalent to two drives. Half the storage capacity is usable.
\Zb    Redundancy = can lose 1 drive per mirror pair.
\Zb
\Zb* ZFS RAIDZ offers good redundancy and storage efficiency but reduced write performance.
\Zb    Read performance is decent.
\Zb
\ZbStorage usage:
\Zb- RAIDZ: ~67% usable (3 drives)
\Zb    * Redundancy = can lose 1 drive.
\Zb
\Zb- RAIDZ2: ~60% usable (5 drives)
\Zb    * Redundancy = can lose 2 drives.
\Zb
\Zb- RAIDZ3: ~57% usable (7 drives)
\Zb    * Redundancy = can lose 3 drives.
\Zb
\ZbTip 1:
\Zb    Take in consideration that mix NVME slots that are directly connected to the CPU and NVME slots
\Zb    that are connected to the motherboards chiplets can have negative impact on performance.
\Zb
\Zb    My own testing showed that the chiplet connected NVME slots with CPU connected NVME slots
\Zb    will throttle the performance above 10-15 GB/s
\Zb
\ZbTip 2:
\Zb    Nordix is a performance oriented distribution and it is designed to use NVME, SSD, and HDD
\Zb    as storage space, RAM is workspace, take that in consideration, the zpool setup should be
\Zb    optimized to feed ARC as fast as possible. Read matters most.
\Zb
\Zb#==================================================================================================#
\Zb#               Recommendations: Stripe/mirror for Home PC, RAIDZ for Servers                      #
\Zb#==================================================================================================#
\Zb
\ZbZFS is designed for enterprise environments with many drives. For home PCs with few drives:
\Zb  - Stripe is a viable option for maximum performance and full storage utilization
\Zb  - ZFS will close the pool on serious errors (read-only import may still be possible)
\Zb  - Nordix have S.M.A.R.T monitoring service activated natively to help detect drive failures early
\Zb  - Mirror or RAIDZ are recommended if you need redundancy
\Zb
\ZbKeep experimenting with different configurations:
\Zb  - Use ZFS send/receive for backups before testing new setups
\Zb  - Restore from backup if something goes wrong
\Zb  - The best configuration is the one you learn the most from!
\Zb
\Zb#==================================================================================================#
\Zb#                     Device Compatibility Notes                                                   #
\Zb#==================================================================================================#
\ZbWhen mixing drives in a vdev:
\Zb
\Zb* HDDs: Mixing different speeds (5400 RPM vs 7200 RPM) is generally fine - the impact is minor.
\Zb  But if you want to run the operating system from an HDD,
\Zb  mixing different speeds can cause bottlenecks.
\Zb
\Zb* SSDs/NVMe: Mixing generations can cause bottlenecks:
\Zb  - NVMe Gen3 + Gen4/Gen5 in stripe: Gen4/Gen5 drives will be limited to Gen3 speeds
\Zb  - NVMe Gen4 + Gen5 in stripe: Gen5 drives will be limited to Gen4 speeds
\Zb  - SATA SSDs: Usually similar speeds so mixing is less of an issue
\Zb
\ZbRecommendation:
\Zb- Best scenario: Use identical drives for best performance and reliability.
\Zb- Good practice: Use devices with the same capacity and speed when mixing in a vdev.
\Zb- Avoid mixing drives from different generations in a vdev.
\Zb- Dont mix HDDs, SSDs and NVMe in the same vdev!
\Zb
\Zb#==================================================================================================#
\Zb#                              PCIe Topology and NVMe Performance                                  #
\Zb#==================================================================================================#
\ZbNote: PCIe lanes can be routed differently depending on your motherboard design:
\Zb- CPU-direct lanes: Direct connection to CPU, lowest latency
\Zb- PCH/chiplet lanes: Through platform controller hub, slightly higher latency
\Zb
\ZbThis matters most for performance-focused setups:
\Zb- Stripe/mirror with multiple NVMe: CPU-lanes give best throughput and latency
\Zb- RAIDZ with HDDs or single NVMe: PCIe topology has minimal impact (parity/disk speed dominates)
\Zb
\ZbFor redundancy-focused systems (RAIDZ, mirror):
\Zb- The difference between CPU/PCH lanes is negligible compared to disk performance
\Zb- Focus more on drive count and capacity than slot placement
\Zb
\ZbCheck your motherboard manual to identify which NVMe slots are CPU-connected for optimal setups.
\Zb
\Zb
